WEBVTT

1
00:00:04.400 --> 00:00:10.251
Repeat four times: brush the top, brush
the bottom. Round and round it goes.

2
00:00:10.471 --> 00:00:14.744
That is a loop. A loop can count
its turns, or never stop at all.

3
00:00:15.244 --> 00:00:19.052
A loop is a part of an algorithm
that happens again and again.

4
00:00:19.272 --> 00:00:25.495
These three steps water one plant. Fill
the can, pour on a plant, walk to the next.

5
00:00:25.715 --> 00:00:30.359
When you reach the last step in
the loop, you go back to the first.

6
00:00:30.579 --> 00:00:34.526
And you keep count of the turns.
This loop goes round three times.

7
00:00:35.026 --> 00:00:39.717
Repeat four times: brush the top,
brush the bottom. Then rinse.

8
00:00:39.937 --> 00:00:44.674
A count-controlled loop has something
you cannot see. It has a counter.

9
00:00:44.894 --> 00:00:48.748
The counter starts at one, and
goes up by one every time round.

10
00:00:48.968 --> 00:00:52.776
When the counter reaches four,
the loop stops. Then you rinse.

11
00:00:52.996 --> 00:00:56.061
That is iteration with
a count-controlled loop.

12
00:00:56.281 --> 00:00:59.671
Two steps, four turns. That is
eight brushing steps in all.

13
00:01:00.171 --> 00:01:04.722
Traffic lights run a loop too.
Red, red and amber, green, amber.

14
00:01:04.942 --> 00:01:08.751
The box says forever. There
is no count in it at all.

15
00:01:08.971 --> 00:01:14.079
So the loop has no end of its own.
Something outside has to stop it.

16
00:01:14.299 --> 00:01:19.314
A switch, a person, a power
cut. That is an indefinite loop.

17
00:01:19.534 --> 00:01:24.132
Forever does not mean broken. Clocks
and games are meant to keep going.

18
00:01:24.632 --> 00:01:29.926
A wrong step outside a loop goes wrong
once. Inside, it goes wrong every turn.

19
00:01:30.146 --> 00:01:35.812
Repeat three times: fill the can, pour
on a plant, tip the can upside down.

20
00:01:36.032 --> 00:01:39.561
Tipping the can empties it on
the floor, every time round.

21
00:01:39.781 --> 00:01:42.986
Change that one step to
walk to the next plant.

22
00:01:43.206 --> 00:01:46.874
One fix inside the loop, and
all three turns are fixed.

23
00:01:47.374 --> 00:01:51.833
Fill, pour, walk, three times
over, is nine steps written out.

24
00:01:52.053 --> 00:01:56.464
Repeat three times, fill, pour,
walk, is three steps and a count.

25
00:01:56.684 --> 00:02:00.353
Shorter to read, and shorter to
write. You see it all at once.

26
00:02:00.573 --> 00:02:04.567
Four plants instead of three?
Change the three to a four.

27
00:02:04.787 --> 00:02:09.849
Written out long, you would add
three more steps. A loop is concise.

28
00:02:10.349 --> 00:02:13.785
There is a third kind of loop,
waiting for you in Scratch.

29
00:02:14.005 --> 00:02:18.231
A condition-controlled loop has
no count, but it does have an end.

30
00:02:18.451 --> 00:02:24.349
It goes round until something becomes true.
Repeat until the down arrow is pressed.

31
00:02:24.569 --> 00:02:29.538
One stops after a number, one when
something happens, and forever never stops.

32
00:02:30.038 --> 00:02:33.846
A loop is a part of an algorithm
that happens again and again.

33
00:02:34.066 --> 00:02:38.989
A count-controlled loop has a counter.
It counts its turns, then stops.

34
00:02:39.209 --> 00:02:44.085
A forever loop has no counter, so
something outside has to stop it.

35
00:02:44.305 --> 00:02:47.881
And a loop is concise:
written once, done many times.
